- Alright, I like your club.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.720
Well, you were talking about your daughter

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:25.650
the last time that you were here.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:26.483
Is she two now?

00:00:26.483 --> 00:00:28.343
- She's two years old.

00:00:28.343 --> 00:00:29.176
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:29.176 --> 00:00:30.420
- Yeah, she's two years old.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:31.800
- Is it terrible twos or fun?

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:32.940
Is it fun?

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.223
- Oh, I think it's inspirational twos.

00:00:35.223 --> 00:00:36.720
(Kelly laughing)

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:37.553
I'm just like-

00:00:37.553 --> 00:00:39.150
- That's a very good answer, PC.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:40.647
- Thank you so much, yeah. (chuckling)

00:00:40.647 --> 00:00:41.920
Highly trained.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.020
- Yeah.
- Yeah,

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:43.853
that's what I'm known for.

00:00:43.853 --> 00:00:44.686
(Kelly laughing)

00:00:44.686 --> 00:00:45.630
Total control.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.120
But yeah, she's really confident.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.740
She climbs up everything.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:50.573
- [Kelly] Sure.

00:00:50.573 --> 00:00:52.230
- She was at a pool party this weekend,

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:53.790
and started chanting her own name

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.170
and made everyone else do it.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:56.003
- [Brendon] What's her name?

00:00:56.003 --> 00:00:58.007
- Love it.
- Her name is Ida.

00:00:58.007 --> 00:01:01.007
- [Kelly And Brendon] Ida, Ida, Ida!

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.010
- I love that she's
like her own hype girl.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:05.843
That's awesome.

00:01:05.843 --> 00:01:07.087
- Yeah, I mean, I was like,
- It's cool.

00:01:07.087 --> 00:01:09.732
"I wish I could be like that now."

00:01:09.732 --> 00:01:10.830
(Kelly laughing)

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:11.910
Yeah, yeah.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.640
- Wait, you were also raising
chickens the last time I saw.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:15.780
How are the chickens?

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.760
- Okay, this is a weird thing to say,

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.620
but they were a rental.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:20.453
- [Kelly] What?

00:01:20.453 --> 00:01:21.440
- Yeah. They-
- What?

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.030
- Because when I got the
chickens, I was like,

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.597
I'll just be this kind
of person who's like,

00:01:25.597 --> 00:01:28.168
"Hey, I'm earthy. I have chickens."

00:01:28.168 --> 00:01:29.124
(Kelly laughing)

00:01:29.124 --> 00:01:31.233
But really, my husband was like,

00:01:32.107 --> 00:01:35.100
"You're not going to want
these chickens for very long."

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.960
And there was a guy near us,

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.760
we live in Massachusetts,
most of the year.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:39.930
And he rents chickens.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.270
Kind of like, why not?

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.100
Memorial Day to Labor Day.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.960
And, yeah.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:46.793
- [Kelly] That's interesting.

00:01:46.793 --> 00:01:48.058
- We got rid of those girls.

00:01:48.058 --> 00:01:49.211
They were really weird.

00:01:49.211 --> 00:01:51.900
- Yeah, those hens were
hard to handle, okay.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.030
- Those hens were following
my husband around everywhere

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.820
and I was like, I almost
got a little bit jealous.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:57.930
(Kelly laughing)

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:00.210
Like they didn't want to
have anything to do with me

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:01.710
and they just followed my husband around.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:02.543
And then-

00:02:02.543 --> 00:02:03.420
- Did you like having eggs though?

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.440
That's kind of cool.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:05.681
- Here's the thing.
- Oh!

00:02:05.681 --> 00:02:09.960
- We were getting like
one egg every four days.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:10.793
- What?

00:02:10.793 --> 00:02:11.640
- They just were weird

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.070
and they slept on the top of their coup.

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:14.903
- Lazy chickens.

00:02:14.903 --> 00:02:16.680
- Lazy, lazy chickens.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:17.910
Pop them out, girls.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:19.110
What the heck?

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:19.943
- I want some eggs.

00:02:19.943 --> 00:02:21.450
- [Jenny] I want some eggs, yeah.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.670
- You probably had no ticks
in the backyard, right?

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.554
- Oh, I wish. I mean, not to-

00:02:25.554 --> 00:02:26.840
- Lazy chickens.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.010
- I know, not to be a bummer, but yeah.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.840
That's a got, a big tick
problem in Massachusetts.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.270
- Well, the Northeast, yeah.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:34.800
- [Kelly] In Texas as
well, where I'm from.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:35.940
Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.740
Well, have you ever had any chickens?

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.360
I know everybody's like
checking themselves.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:40.740
- Did you see, I just did that too.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:41.820
- Everyone itches.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.010
Have you ever had any
like, not odd animals,

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.150
but farm animals or anything like that?

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:46.260
Other than like a dog or cat?

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:47.250
- I've had a horse.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:48.083
- [Kelly] Oh, okay.

00:02:48.083 --> 00:02:49.500
- He was very important to me.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.353
I met him making a film in Mexico.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.204
We bonded.

00:02:55.204 --> 00:02:56.310
I couldn't say goodbye,

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:01.310
so I had him sent up from Durango
to past Suarez to El Paso.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:06.300
He got on a FedEx and he flew,
flew him to New York City.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:07.530
- A horse flew FedEx.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.360
- [Brendon] Yes. That's the
easiest way to get him there.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.220
- They really will deliver everything.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.053
- Correct.

00:03:12.053 --> 00:03:13.260
- Yeah.
- They will.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.890
And he was a great
companion to my oldest son

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.320
who has an intellectual disability.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:20.153
He's autistic.

00:03:20.153 --> 00:03:21.930
And so even if he didn't want a ride,

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.730
you could give him a brush and a bucket.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:24.840
And you know, they're both happy,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.790
all afternoon, you know?
- No, it's very therapeutic.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:27.623
They do that all the time.

00:03:27.623 --> 00:03:28.560
- Oh, ab-
- Yeah.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:29.393
- [Kelly] Equine therapy.

00:03:29.393 --> 00:03:30.540
- Absolutely.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:32.220
- Yeah. That's cool.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:34.800
Well, congrats on the Oscar nomination.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:35.633
- Oh, thank you.

00:03:35.633 --> 00:03:36.466
- Yeah.

00:03:36.466 --> 00:03:37.320
- I just was looking at him.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.603
- Well, both of you are Oscar nominated.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.120
It's awesome. You're both nominated.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.040
So this is also for a
best animated feature.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.650
So tell us about the movie and
how it felt to get nominated.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:47.550
- Sure.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:50.070
The movie is called "Marcel
the Shell with Shoes On."

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:53.250
It's about a one-eyed
shell who has two shoes.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:54.900
There he is!
(Kelly laughing)

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.870
And it's directed by Dean Fleischer Camp.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:00.540
And we wrote it together
with our friend, Nick Paley.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.720
And it's about a little
guy who mysteriously,

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.100
sort of, at the start
of the film at least,

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.510
loses his family

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:09.870
and meets a documentarian
who starts to kind of,

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.510
chronicle his story and
then they get an opportunity

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:14.220
to perhaps find the family.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.970
And it's about that
process of figuring out

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:19.860
if you even wanna go on an adventure

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:21.450
and I think it's very lovely,
- Yeah.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:22.890
very close to the heart.

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:26.820
Yeah. And finding out that we
were nominated for an Oscar.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:27.653
It was a,

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:29.850
a trip.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.190
- Yeah. Like, wait, what?

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:36.710
- Yeah, it really was, I have
nothing to compare it to.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:38.820
It was not even anything
I've ever dreamed of.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.010
Like I just always really
wanted to be an actor.

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:43.230
That was like, that's
definitely good enough for me

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:45.840
to just be chosen to play
a role and have a camera.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.250
You know, like, I just wanted that.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:51.660
And it was probably like a
little past four in the morning

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.940
and I was getting all
these texts on my phone

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:57.030
and I knew it was Oscar
nomination day, and I was like,

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.340
I kind of think someone would've called.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:01.830
You know, like I think these
might be apology texts.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:03.637
- Like, "Oh, sorry, it didn't happen."

00:05:03.637 --> 00:05:04.620
"Maybe next time."

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:05.730
- Totally, totally.

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:07.680
And then, and so I was like, meh,

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.150
I kind of rolled over in bed,

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:10.920
and then someone, my friend Liz,

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:13.260
who produced the film called and said

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:15.480
that we've been nominated for an Oscar.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:16.500
- [Kelly] That's so cool.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:17.960
- Yeah.
(audience cheering)

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:18.793
- That's so cool.

00:05:18.793 --> 00:05:20.520
So you paid tribute to your grandmother's,

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:21.397
both of them, right?

00:05:21.397 --> 00:05:22.230
Isn't it?
- Yes.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:24.600
- I love that. Why was
this important to you?

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.330
- You know, my grandmothers
are just like the

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:30.180
some of the closest relationships
I've ever experienced.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:31.230
- [Kelly] That's wonderful.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:32.580
- Yeah. It's, it's lovely.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.670
And my grandmother, Rochelle,
recently passed and-

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:36.503
- I'm sorry.

00:05:36.503 --> 00:05:38.280
- [Jenny] Yeah, that was really hard.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.640
And the grandmother in the
film is named Nana Connie,

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.261
that's my other grandmother's name.

00:05:43.261 --> 00:05:45.930
The grandmother in the film
has a beautiful accent.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:46.793
There she is.

00:05:46.793 --> 00:05:47.626
- [Brendon] Aww.

00:05:47.626 --> 00:05:49.050
- [Jenny] She's played
by Isabella Rossellini,

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:50.610
who has a, a gorgeous accent.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:53.730
And my grandmother, Rochelle
was born in Havana, Cuba,

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:56.970
in 1930, and then moved to Paris in about,

00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:01.755
I don't know, maybe 1933 or
34 and our family is Jewish.

00:06:01.755 --> 00:06:04.950
They basically ran right
into Second World War.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:07.140
She became, she was a Holocaust survivor.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:07.973
- Wow.

00:06:07.973 --> 00:06:08.940
- She had a gorgeous accent,

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:10.440
an incredible outlook on life.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.810
And that all went into that character.

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:14.400
I think my father might have described her

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.260
as like an artist without a genre.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:17.910
And just super dreamy.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:18.743
- [Brendon] I love that.

00:06:18.743 --> 00:06:19.680
- Yeah, yeah.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.390
- Wow. Your family's magical.

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:22.230
- I think so.

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:23.160
- Okay.
- Yeah.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:24.480
- [Kelly] Yeah. That's nice.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:25.980
Okay, well, we have a clip.

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:28.320
This is when Marcel is about to leave home

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:29.520
in search of his family

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.260
and he is getting his grandmother prepared

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:33.690
to live without him for a while.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:34.523
- Alright!

00:06:34.523 --> 00:06:36.630
So you've got the book, you've
got a little bit of a raisin,

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:37.920
which I really don't
think you're gonna finish,

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:38.753
but you can go to town.

00:06:38.753 --> 00:06:39.810
Go to town.

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:42.030
Also, you have two drips
of wa- Look over here.

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:43.140
You have two drips of water.

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:46.620
You got an LED flashlight
in case the power goes out.

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:48.000
You just gotta step on it like this.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:48.840
See? See how-

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:49.920
Whoa, sorry.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:50.880
Don't stand right in front of it.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.040
Finally, now, this is the sparkler.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:54.870
What you do is you light this end on fire

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:55.920
and then you step back.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:57.930
This can act as a flare, right?

00:06:57.930 --> 00:06:58.763
- What would I need?

00:06:58.763 --> 00:07:00.330
- That's if you need a signal for help

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:03.135
and Dean's going to leave
you his phone just in case.

00:07:03.135 --> 00:07:03.968
- What? I don't know him.

00:07:03.968 --> 00:07:04.801
- You know what?

00:07:04.801 --> 00:07:05.790
Now that I'm thinking about it

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:08.790
why don't we just put the
colander over you now if you want.

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:12.270
- [Marcel] How's that?

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.068
- You must let me out of here.

00:07:14.068 --> 00:07:15.363
- Just let me out of here.

00:07:15.363 --> 00:07:17.190
(crowd applaud)

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:20.730
So, "Marcel the Shell with
Shoes On" is nominated

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:22.710
for an Oscar for best animated feature.

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:24.513
You can stream it now on demand.

